---
/**
 * Unix Timestamp to SGT — 싱가포르 영어판 다섯 번째 도구(유닉스 타임스탬프 변환). 한국어판 /tools/unix-time/ 와
 * 계산(lib/unix-time.ts)·스크립트(scripts/unix-time.ts 의 EN 사전 — 날짜 표기는 <html lang="en-SG"> 로 일·월 순)를
 * 같이 쓰고, 문장은 싱가포르 영어로 새로 썼다(번역 아님). 조사표 싱가포르 칸 '같음'(POSIX 4.19). 고친 공유 파일 없음.
 *
 * 소제목은 싱가포르 구글 자동완성에서 왔다(keywords/sg-unix-time-2026-10-21.csv, hl en · gl sg):
 *   unix timestamp to sgt · epoch time to sgt · unix time to singapore time / epoch time now · unix timestamp now /
 *   unix timestamp meaning · format · milliseconds / epoch converter in excel / timestamp converter discord /
 *   unix timestamp to utc · to datetime
 * 시간대는 UTC 와 '이 기기 시간대'만 — 싱가포르 표준시 오프셋은 허용 출처(IRAS·MOM·CPF·SSO)에서 확인하지 않아
 * UTC+8 같은 숫자를 쓰지 않는다. 본문 예시 숫자는 전부 아래 frontmatter 에서 lib 함수로 계산한다.
 */
import BaseHead from "../../../../components/BaseHead.astro";
import AdSlot from "../../../../components/AdSlot.astro";
import ClarityHead from "../../../../components/ClarityHead.astro";
import GtagHead from "../../../../components/GtagHead.astro";
import TrackLinks from "../../../../components/TrackLinks.astro";
import AdSenseHead from "../../../../components/AdSenseHead.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../../../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import "../../../../styles/global.css";
import { INT32_MAX, toParts, enText, isoText, secondsText, dateValue, timeValue } from "../../../../lib/unix-time";

const LANG = "en-SG";
const title = "Unix Timestamp to SGT — Epoch Time Converter Singapore";
const description =
  "Convert a Unix timestamp (10-digit seconds or 13-digit ms) to a date in UTC or Singapore time on your device, or a date to epoch time. Excel and Discord tips.";

/** 처음 보이는 예 — 2026-10-19 03:34:56 UTC */
const BASE_MS = 1_792_380_896_000;
const p0 = toParts(BASE_MS, 0);
const BASE_S = secondsText(BASE_MS);
const zero = enText(toParts(0, 0), LANG);
const minus1 = enText(toParts(-1000, 0), LANG);
const y2038 = enText(toParts(INT32_MAX * 1000, 0), LANG);
const wrap = enText(toParts(-(INT32_MAX + 1) * 1000, 0), LANG);
---

<html lang="en-SG">
  <head>
    <BaseHead title={title} description={description} />
    <ClarityHead />
    <GtagHead />
    <AdSenseHead />
  </head>
  <body class="bg-texture">
    <SiteHeader />
    <main>
      <h1>Unix Timestamp Converter (Epoch Time ⇄ Date)</h1>
      <p>
        An app log, a payment record or an API response hands you something like {BASE_S} and you need to know what time that
        was. Paste the number in and you get the date and time; type a date and time instead and you get the timestamp back.
        Change either side and the other follows at once.
      </p>

      <div class="calc-card" data-two>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="zone">Time zone</label>
          <select id="zone" data-seg>
            <option value="utc" selected>UTC</option>
            <option value="device">Your time zone</option>
          </select>
          <p class="hint" id="zone-hint">The date fields and the result use this time zone. The timestamp itself doesn't change.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="ts">Unix timestamp</label>
          <input type="text" id="ts" inputmode="decimal" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" value={BASE_S} placeholder="e.g. 1704067200" />
          <p class="hint" id="ts-hint">10 digits are read as seconds, 13 digits as milliseconds.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="unit">Unit</label>
          <select id="unit" data-seg>
            <option value="auto" selected>Auto</option>
            <option value="s">Seconds</option>
            <option value="ms">Milliseconds</option>
          </select>
        </div>

        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="date">Date</label>
          <input type="date" id="date" value={dateValue(p0)} min="1900-01-01" max="9999-12-30" />
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="time">Time (24-hour, hh:mm:ss)</label>
          <input type="time" id="time" step="1" value={timeValue(p0)} />
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="msec">Milliseconds (0–999)</label>
          <input type="number" id="msec" inputmode="numeric" min="0" max="999" step="1" value="0" />
        </div>

        <div class="actions">
          <button id="btn-now" type="button" class="secondary">Now</button>
        </div>

        <div class="result-hero" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true" data-dock="hero-value">
          <p class="label" id="hero-label">UTC</p>
          <p class="big" id="hero-value">{enText(p0, LANG)}</p>
          <p class="hint" id="hero-sub"></p>
        </div>

        <div class="stat-row" data-receipt id="receipt">
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label">Seconds (s)</span>
            <span class="value mono" id="v-sec">{BASE_S}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label">Milliseconds (ms)</span>
            <span class="value mono" id="v-ms">{BASE_MS}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label">ISO 8601 (UTC)</span>
            <span class="value mono" id="v-iso">{isoText(BASE_MS, 0)}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label">ISO 8601 (selected time zone)</span>
            <span class="value mono" id="v-iso-local">{isoText(BASE_MS, 0)}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label">From now</span>
            <span class="value" id="v-rel">—</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="hint" id="note"></p>
        <p class="meta" id="now-line">Now: —</p>

        <div class="actions">
          <button id="btn-download" type="button">Save as image</button>
          <button id="btn-share" type="button" class="secondary">Share</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Unix timestamp to SGT (Singapore time)</h2>
      <p>
        A timestamp has no time zone of its own — {BASE_S} is the same instant in Singapore, London or New York. What changes is
        how that instant is written as a clock time. The converter always shows it in <strong>UTC</strong> first. Choose
        <strong>Your time zone</strong> and it switches to whatever zone your phone or laptop is set to; if that's Singapore, you
        are reading SGT, and the offset from UTC is shown next to the result. Dates you type in are read in the same zone, so
        "9 am in Singapore" turns into the right timestamp. Want another city's time? Change the time zone in your device settings
        and reload.
      </p>

      <h2>What a Unix timestamp means</h2>
      <p>
        It's a count of <strong>seconds since 00:00:00 UTC on 1 January 1970</strong>, a moment known as the epoch. The POSIX
        standard (IEEE Std 1003.1) defines the count: every day is treated as exactly 86,400 seconds, and leap seconds are not
        counted. So 0 is {zero} UTC, and −1,000 milliseconds is {minus1} UTC.
      </p>

      <h2>Epoch time now</h2>
      <p>
        Below the converter, the <em>Now</em> line ticks over every second with the current timestamp in seconds and
        milliseconds. Press <strong>Now</strong> to drop the current moment into the converter. Both come from your device's
        clock, so if the clock is off, so is the reading.
      </p>

      <h2>Format: seconds or milliseconds?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>10 digits, like {BASE_S}</strong> — seconds. Every moment from September 2001 to November 2286 fits in
          10 digits.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>13 digits, like {BASE_MS}</strong> — milliseconds. This is what JavaScript's <code>Date.now()</code> and
          Java's <code>System.currentTimeMillis()</code> give you. Divide by 1,000 to get seconds.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What Auto does</strong> — a number with a decimal point is taken as seconds; a whole number of up to
          11 digits is seconds, and 12 digits or more is milliseconds. Commas and spaces are skipped. If the guess is wrong, set
          the unit yourself.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Epoch converter in Excel or Google Sheets</h2>
      <p>
        Spreadsheets count dates as days, with one day equal to 1. So divide the seconds by 86,400 and add the epoch date. With
        a timestamp in cell A1:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Seconds to a UTC date and time: <code>=A1/86400+DATE(1970,1,1)</code></li>
        <li>Milliseconds (13 digits): <code>=A1/86400000+DATE(1970,1,1)</code></li>
        <li>Singapore or any local time: <code>=A1/86400+DATE(1970,1,1)+(offset/24)</code>, where <em>offset</em> is the number of hours the converter shows beside the local result</li>
        <li>A UTC date and time back to seconds: <code>=(A1-DATE(1970,1,1))*86400</code></li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If the cell shows a plain number, apply a date-time format such as <code>dd/mm/yyyy hh:mm:ss</code>.
      </p>

      <h2>Unix timestamp to datetime in code</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Python</strong>: <code>datetime.fromtimestamp({BASE_S}, tz=timezone.utc)</code> (with <code>from datetime import datetime, timezone</code>)</li>
        <li><strong>pandas</strong>: <code>pd.to_datetime({BASE_S}, unit="s")</code> — <code>unit="ms"</code> for milliseconds</li>
        <li><strong>JavaScript</strong>: <code>new Date({BASE_S} * 1000)</code> — seconds must be multiplied by 1,000 first</li>
        <li><strong>MySQL</strong>: <code>SELECT FROM_UNIXTIME({BASE_S});</code></li>
        <li><strong>PostgreSQL</strong>: <code>SELECT to_timestamp({BASE_S});</code></li>
        <li><strong>Linux / macOS shell</strong>: <code>date +%s</code> prints the timestamp now; GNU <code>date -d @{BASE_S}</code> turns one into a date</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Paste the same number into the converter above to check what your code prints.</p>

      <h2>Discord timestamp converter</h2>
      <p>
        Put <code>&lt;t:{BASE_S}:F&gt;</code> in a Discord message and each reader sees that moment in their own time zone — useful
        when you're in Singapore and your teammates or raid group are spread across other countries. The letter after the second
        colon sets the style: <code>t</code>/<code>T</code> short and long time, <code>d</code>/<code>D</code> short and long
        date, <code>f</code>/<code>F</code> date with time, and <code>R</code> relative ("in 3 days"). Use seconds, not
        milliseconds.
      </p>

      <h2>The year 2038 limit</h2>
      <p>
        A signed 32-bit integer tops out at 2,147,483,647, which as a timestamp is {y2038} UTC. One second later, software that
        still keeps time in a signed 32-bit field overflows and jumps back to {wrap} UTC. The converter adds a note under the
        result for any time beyond that point.
      </p>

      <details>
        <summary>Can it show dates before 1970?</summary>
        <p>
          Yes — they're negative numbers. −1 is 31 December 1969 23:59:59 UTC, one second before the epoch. POSIX writes its
          formula for 1970 onwards, but this converter, like most programming languages, keeps counting backwards at
          86,400 seconds a day. The range is 1 January 1900 to the year 9999.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Does it accept decimals, microseconds or nanoseconds?</summary>
        <p>
          A decimal such as 1792380896.789 is read as seconds with up to three decimal places (milliseconds). Microsecond and
          nanosecond values (16 and 19 digits) aren't supported — divide by 1,000 or 1,000,000 first to get milliseconds.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Why is the time off by several hours?</summary>
        <p>
          Almost always a time zone mix-up: the number was read as UTC when you expected Singapore time, or the other way round.
          Flip between <strong>UTC</strong> and <strong>Your time zone</strong> to compare. If the year comes out as 1970 or far in
          the future, the unit is off by a factor of 1,000 — pick seconds or milliseconds yourself.
        </p>
      </details>

      <p>
        For the number of days between two dates, try the <a href="/en-sg/tools/date-diff/">date calculator</a>; to count down to
        a date, the <a href="/en-sg/tools/dday/">days until countdown</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Source: <a href="https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/basedefs/V1_chap04.html" rel="noopener">The Open Group Base Specifications Issue 8 (IEEE Std 1003.1-2024), 4.19 Seconds Since the Epoch</a> (checked 21 October 2026)
      </p>

      <AdSlot label="unix-time-result-en-sg" />
    </main>
    <SiteFooter />
    <style>
      .mono { font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
      #hero-value { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
    </style>
    <script src="../../../../scripts/unix-time.ts"></script>
    <TrackLinks />
    <script src="../../../../scripts/calc-ui.ts"></script>
  </body>
</html>
